# META-PREFIX-DECODE: a person's two Escapes, and two Escape bytes in one
# burst, at an idle draft and with a menu open. fx v0.0.16 reads a second
# ESC that comes before the first's quiet as its meta prefix: no event, and
# the pair is one Escape when the quiet comes (escape_parser.zig:552-555,
# terminal_action_decoder.zig:84-88, :139, :174-183@481104fd). A person's
# presses, a quiet apart, are two and do what two do: at a draft the second
# clears it (app_input_runtime.zig:2788-2799), and a menu's Escape closes the
# menu and arms nothing (:2775-2779). `key Escape Escape` is one tmux write,
# so the binary reads both bytes at once; the person's presses are two
# writes, the second sent once the first's hint is on the screen.
# (a) An idle draft, the person's two presses: the draft is cleared.
type alpha one
until shown alpha one
key Escape
until shown esc again to clear
key Escape
until gone esc again to clear
snap a-person-cleared
# (b) The two bytes in one burst: one press, which arms the clear and keeps
# the draft; snapped past the clear's 500 ms (gesture_state.zig:4).
type beta two
until shown beta two
key Escape Escape
wait 1
snap b-burst-kept
# (c) A menu, `/he`'s completions. The person's first press closes it and
# arms nothing; the second, at the draft, arms the clear; the third clears.
key C-u
type /he
wait 1
snap c-menu
key Escape
wait 0.7
snap c-person-closed
key Escape
until shown esc again to clear
key Escape
until gone esc again to clear
snap c-person-cleared
# (d) The menu again, and the two bytes in one burst: one press, which closes
# it. A person's press 0.3 s later is then the first at the draft, which
# arms and keeps it -- where a burst read as two presses would have armed
# the clear already, and this press would clear the draft.
type /he
wait 1
key Escape Escape
wait 0.3
key Escape
wait 1
snap d-burst-kept
